    "rationale": "Evidence: the iteration-0 design (kp 2.5, base 1.9, weights -2..2, 16 mm sensor spacing) raced 60 trials and failed on every track. Every run ended off track at max_error about 0.061 m, with one line-loss event each time, and steering_oscillation was small (0.064 to 0.075). The failure is therefore a consistent lateral drift, not a growing oscillation. The line-loss mechanism explains the end: with 16 mm spacing and 12 mm footprints, the outermost sensor (x = 0.032) stops seeing tape at about 0.05 m offset, so all sensors drop out just before the 0.06 m off-track limit. Line loss then drives the robot off with throttle 0.35.\n\nCritique: I accept that the draft's 'halved gain' was wrong to say the wheel_track change reduces gain. I keep wheel_track at 0.12, so yaw per turn unit is unchanged. I also accept that base_speed 1.4 made completion unlikely. I reject the claim that the iteration-0 result shows no steering problem. The data shows a constant departure, and that is what I now target.\n\nChanges: (1) Sensor span widened to x = -0.06 to +0.06 at 0.03 m spacing, with sensor_size 0.016. Coverage stays continuous (16 mm footprints, 14 mm gaps, 25 mm tape). Line loss now begins only at about 0.08 m offset, beyond the 0.06 m limit, so the controller gets to act on the line before it is lost. (2) Spacing doubled, so signal per metre halves for the same weights. kp raised from 2.5 to 5.0 to keep the same effective steering gain per metre. (3) ki raised from 0.1 to 0.3 to remove the fixed motor-mismatch bias. Integral is capped at 2, so the contribution is at most 0.6 turn units, a bounded correction. (4) kd set to 0.05 (from 0.03). Edge kicks are now about half-size per edge, so this is a small damping term. (5) base_speed set to 1.7, between the 1.6 risk and the 1.9 that failed, to leave headroom for completion without raising drift. Kept: weights (-2,-1,0,1,2), y = 0.06, body, mass, wheel_track 0.12, wheel_radius 0.03, line_loss last_direction.\n\nExpected result (hypothesis, to be tested): max_error stays below 0.06 on the development tracks and line_loss_events drops to 0 or 1. If rms_error rises while steering_oscillation stays low, the cause is bias and ki or kp should rise further. If steering_oscillation rises, lower kp first.",
